      "type": "引用",
      "type_label": "引用",
      "text": "Unfortunately, we are being blocked by certain oligarchs in order to maintain their monopolistic chokehold on the Indian people. You can guess who they are …\n\nThis is a crime against the people of India!",
      "quote": {
        "author": "cb_doge",
        "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1498070100393754625/C2V-fbll_200x200.jpg",
        "created_at_shanghai": "2026-10-07 23:16:46",
        "created_at_utc": "2026-10-07T15:16:46.000Z",
        "id": "2107852643205058819",
        "images": [
          "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2107851792956727296/img/tEGs2aQAAmLIQZPy.jpg"
        ],
        "media": [
          {
            "duration": 11.8,
            "height": 1080,
            "thumbnail_url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2107851792956727296/img/tEGs2aQAAmLIQZPy.jpg",
            "type": "video",
            "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2107851792956727296/vid/avc1/1080x1080/YnP0ZLKchq8OXRbe.mp4?tag=29",
            "width": 1080
          }
        ],
        "name": "DogeDesigner",
        "text": "India needs @Starlink 🇮🇳\n\n• 11,256 of India’s listed villages still lacked 4G coverage as of May 2026.\n\n• India had 1.093 billion internet subscriptions in March 2026, but only 440.87 million were rural. Rural subscription density was 48.31 per 100 people, compared with 126.80 in urban India.\n\n• BharatNet had laid over 850,000 route-km of fibre and made about 221,000 Gram Panchayats service-ready. However, its Points of Presence were reported operational at only 80,472 Gram Panchayats in June 2026.\n\n• Only 4.26% of India’s internet subscriptions were wired connections in March 2026. Starlink could give underserved communities another route to high-speed broadband.\n\n• Starlink has already built 20 gateway sites in India, according to SpaceX Vice President of Starlink Business Operations Lauren Dreyer, speaking at India Mobile Congress today.\n\n• Starlink says it has developed India-specific security controls designed to keep Indian user data within India.\n\n• Its satellite network could bring high-speed internet to remote villages, mountains and islands where extending fibre or mobile networks is difficult.\n\n• Better connectivity could help rural schools access online learning, clinics offer remote consultations and small businesses reach more customers. Starlink already supports education and healthcare in remote communities internationally.\n\n• Portable Starlink terminals have supported disaster response around the world. They could give Indian emergency teams backup connectivity when floods, earthquakes or storms damage ground networks.\n\n• Direct to Cell could also help ordinary 4G phones connect beyond tower coverage, subject to Indian approvals and mobile operator partnerships.\n\n• The government itself identifies difficult terrain, low commercial viability and infrastructure constraints as reasons for remaining coverage gaps. This is exactly where Starlink could help India reach more people.\n\n• India should complete the remaining clearances and spectrum allocation so Starlink can begin commercial service.\n\n• SpaceX’s Vice President of Starlink Business Operations, Lauren Dreyer, said that Starlink is ready to help achieve Prime Minister Modi’s vision of 100% connectivity across India. She highlighted its potential to connect remote classrooms, support telemedicine and restore connectivity after disasters, adding, “With Starlink, people can get connected within minutes, not years.”\n\nIndia’s hardest-to-reach communities deserve reliable internet too.",
        "url": "https://x.com/cb_doge/status/2107852643205058819",
        "verified": true,
        "verified_type": "individual"
      },
