      "text": "Starlink Mobile (direct-to-cell) V2 satellites are incredible. \n\nThis @SpaceX constellation will enable more than 100 times the bandwidth of our current V1 system!",
      "quote": {
        "author": "XFreeze",
        "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1876785200010539008/2_HFJjq9_200x200.jpg",
        "created_at_shanghai": "2026-10-07 23:12:09",
        "created_at_utc": "2026-10-07T15:12:09.000Z",
        "id": "2107851480967618562",
        "images": [
          "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HUCXvy5bMAADf-L.jpg?name=small"
        ],
        "media": [
          {
            "height": 2400,
            "type": "photo",
            "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HUCXvy5bMAADf-L.jpg?name=small",
            "width": 3800
          }
        ],
        "name": "X Freeze",
        "text": "SpaceX just got a massive FCC green light for Starlink Mobile\n\nThe FCC has approved SpaceX to launch and operate up to 15,000 next-generation satellites built for direct-to-phone connectivity\n\nThe new system is designed to:\n\n• Target peak speeds of up to 150 Mbps per user\n• Connect ordinary cell phones directly from space\n• Operate in very low orbit at roughly 326–335 km\n• Use T-Mobile spectrum alongside spectrum SpaceX is acquiring from EchoStar\n• Deliver orders of magnitude more capacity than the first-generation Starlink Mobile constellation\n\nSpaceX currently has roughly 650 dedicated mobile satellites in orbit\n\nThe FCC also granted a waiver giving SpaceX a path to provide satellite wireless service without requiring a spectrum-leasing agreement with a terrestrial mobile operator\n\nStarlink Mobile is moving far beyond basic dead-zone connectivity\n\nSpaceX is building toward a full 5G cellular network in space",
        "url": "https://x.com/XFreeze/status/2107851480967618562",
        "verified": true,
        "verified_type": "individual"
      },
